Title: THE CALL OF THE WILD
Credit: Written by
Author: Jack London
Source: Adapted from the novel by Jack London
Draft date: 7/31/2026

FADE IN:

EXT. SANTA CLARA VALLEY - JUDGE MILLER'S ESTATE - DAY

Sunlight glints on the wide veranda of Judge Miller's spacious California estate.

BUCK, a magnificent 140-pound St. Bernard and Shepherd mix with a thick dark coat and regal posture, trots imperiously across the manicured lawn.

MANUEL, a dark-eyed gardener's helper in dirt-stained clothes, leans against a grape arbor, clutching a newspaper with the bold headline: "GOLD DISCOVERED IN THE KLONDIKE!"

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck did not read the newspapers, or he would have known that trouble was brewing, not alone for himself, but for every tide-water dog, strong of muscle and with warm, long hair, from Puget Sound to San Diego.

EXT. COLLEGE PARK STATION - NIGHT

A solitary lantern hangs over a desolate railway platform. Manuel leads Buck by his leather collar into the gloom.

A STRANGER in a heavy coat steps out of the shadows. Gold coins chink in Manuel's palm.

STRANGER
You might wrap up the goods before you deliver 'm.

Manuel loops a stout rope around Buck's neck, double-twisting it beneath his collar.

MANUEL
Twist it, an' you'll choke 'm plentee.

Buck growls in warning. The Stranger yanks the rope tight, shutting off Buck's breath. Buck rears up in rage, but the Stranger grapples his throat and throws him over on his back. Buck thrashes in agony until his eyes glaze and he goes limp.

INT. EXPRESS CAR - NIGHT

A locomotive whistle SHRIEKS. The car jolts violently along the tracks.

Buck wakes inside a heavy wooden crate, his tongue parched and swollen, throat raw. Bloodshot eyes glare through the wooden slats.

Two EXPRESS MESSENGERS poke sticks through the bars, barking and crowing mockingly at him. Buck hurls his great weight against the bars, frothing with fury.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For two days and nights Buck neither ate nor drank. In his anger he accumulated a fund of wrath that boded ill for whoever first fell foul of him. He was metamorphosed into a raging fiend.

EXT. SEATTLE BACKYARD - DAY

High wooden walls enclose a small muddy yard. Four workmen set down the crate.

MAN IN RED SWEATER, a burly man in a sagging red wool sweater, steps forward carrying a hatchet and a thick wooden club. He jams the hatchet into the crate, prying the top boards apart.

Buck charges through the splintering wood like a launched missile!

The Man in Red Sweater swings the club—CRACK! The heavy blow catches Buck in mid-air, slamming him to the dirt.

Buck rises with a ferocious roar and leaps again.

CRACK! The club smashes him down a second time.

A dozen times Buck charges; a dozen times the club breaks his leap and smashes him down. Blood streams from his nose and ears.

The Man in Red Sweater advances and coolly delivers a crushing blow to Buck's nose. Buck collapses, senseless.

Moments later, Buck stirs. The Man in Red Sweater fearlessly pats Buck's head and sets down a bucket of water. Buck drinks ravenously, then bolts raw meat from the man's hand.

MAN IN RED SWEATER
Answers to the name of Buck. Well, Buck, my boy, we've had our little ruction, and the best thing we can do is to let it go at that. You've learned your place, and I know mine. Be a good dog and all 'll go well. Be a bad dog, and I'll whale the stuffin' outa you. Understand?

PERRAULT, a swarthy French-Canadian government courier, enters the yard and examines Buck's broad chest and strong legs.

PERRAULT
Sacredam! Dat one dam bully dog! Eh? How moch?

MAN IN RED SWEATER
Three hundred, and a present at that. And seem' it's government money, you ain't got no kick coming, eh, Perrault?

Perrault hands over a pouch of coins and clips a leash to Buck, alongside CURLY, a good-natured Newfoundland.

EXT. DYEA BEACH - DAY

Gray sky, freezing gale, and a dark ocean breaking on icy shores. White mushy flakes fall from above.

Buck steps onto the snow, snorting in surprise. He licks a mouthful; it stings like fire.

Curly approaches a native husky in friendly fashion. Without warning, the husky leaps in, teeth clipping like steel, ripping Curly's face open from eye to jaw.

Thirty wild huskies instantly gather in a silent, intent circle, licking their chops.

Curly rushes her attacker; the husky shoulders her off her feet.

The waiting pack leaps upon her. Curly screams beneath a writhing, snapping mass of bodies.

FRANCOIS, a dark half-breed driver, rushes in swinging an axe, joined by Perrault with a club. They beat off the wild pack, but Curly lies limp and shredded in the bloody snow.

SPITZ, a big snow-white husky leader, sits nearby, running out his scarlet tongue in a silent laugh. Buck glares at Spitz with instant, murderous hatred.

Francois fastens heavy leather harness straps onto Buck, hooking him behind Dave and Sol-leks.

FRANCOIS
T'ree vair' good dogs. Dat Buck, heem pool lak hell. I tich heem queek as anyt'ing.

EXT. THIRTY MILE RIVER - DAY

Bitter arctic cold—fifty below zero. A treacherous frozen river with open black water swirling in eddies.

Perrault leads on webbed snowshoes, probing thin ice with a long pole. Francois guides the sled.

Buck pulls in his traces with ferocious energy. He watches PIKE, a clever malingerer, slyly steal a slice of bacon when Perrault's back is turned. The next day, Buck duplicates the trick, snatching a chunk of bacon unnoticed.

At night, cold wind sweeps the white plain. Buck digs a hole deep into a snowdrift. The heat of his body warms the tiny space, and he sleeps snug beneath the snow.

EXT. TAHKEENA CREEK - NIGHT

Pale moonlight floods the frozen creek bed.

A snowshoe rabbit flashes across the snow like a frost wraith.

Buck leads the pack of sixty howling dogs in full cry, body flying low over the ice, whining with the pure joy of the hunt.

Ahead, Spitz cuts across a narrow bend, leaping off a high bank to snap the rabbit's back in mid-air.

The rabbit shrieks. The pack halts, forming a silent, expectant circle in the moonlight.

Buck drives straight at Spitz, shoulder to shoulder!

They roll over in the powdery snow. Spitz leaps up, teeth snapping like a steel trap, slashing Buck's shoulder.

Buck rushes again and again, striving for the white throat, but Spitz counters every blow, slashing Buck until he streams with blood.

The wolfish circle tightens, waiting for the vanquished to fall.

Buck feints a high rush, then sweeps low to the snow.

CRUNCH! Buck's jaws break Spitz's left foreleg.

Spitz, snarling on three legs, struggles to stay upright. Buck swoops low again—CRUNCH! The right foreleg snaps.

Spitz quivers, losing his footing. Buck springs in, shoulder meeting shoulder!

Spitz disappears beneath the dark, surging circle of the pack. Buck stands victorious on the moonlit snow—the dominant primordial beast.

EXT. YUKON TRAIL - DAY

Morning light hits the camp. Spitz is gone.

Francois brings SOL-LEKS to the lead position of the sled.

Buck leaps upon Sol-leks in a fury, driving him back and taking the head of the team.

FRANCOIS
Eh? Look at dat Buck. Heem keel dat Spitz, heem t'ink to take de job. Go 'way, Chook!

Francois advances with a heavy club. Buck retreats slowly, keeping just beyond reach, eyes fixed on the club.

Perrault checks his pocket watch.

PERRAULT
T'row down de club.

Francois drops the club. Buck trots in, laughing as dogs laugh, and swings into position at the head of the team.

Francois hitches the traces.

FRANCOIS
Nevaire such a dog as dat Buck! No, nevaire! Heem worth one t'ousan' dollair, by Gar!

EXT. WHITE RIVER CAMP - DAY

Spring sun blazes overhead. The river ice is soft, cracked, and honeycombed with water holes.

An exhausted, starving dog team staggers into camp. Buck's ribs outline cleanly through his matted hide.

HAL, a callow youth wearing a cartridge belt, revolver, and hunting knife, wields a heavy club. CHARLES, a watery-eyed man, and MERCEDES, a tearful woman, sit atop a massive, overloaded sled.

JOHN THORNTON, a rugged, bearded woodsman, sits on a log, whittling an axe handle.

HAL
They told us up above that the bottom was dropping out of the trail and that the best thing for us to do was to lay over. They told us we couldn't make White River, and here we are.

JOHN THORNTON
And they told you true. The bottom's likely to drop out at any moment. Only fools, with the blind luck of fools, could have made it. I tell you straight, I wouldn't risk my carcass on that ice for all the gold in Alaska.

HAL
That's because you're not a fool, I suppose. All the same, we'll go on to Dawson. Get up there, Buck! Hi! Get up there! Mush on!

Hal lashes the team. The other dogs stagger to their feet, but Buck lies motionless in the snow, sensing catastrophe ahead on the river.

Hal trades his whip for a heavy club and rains brutal blows upon Buck. Buck endures the beating in silence, his spark of life dimming.

Thornton springs up with an animal roar, hurtling Hal backward into the snow!

JOHN THORNTON
If you strike that dog again, I'll kill you.

HAL
It's my dog. Get out of my way, or I'll fix you. I'm going to Dawson.

Hal draws his long hunting knife. Thornton raps Hal's knuckles with the axe handle, dropping the knife, then slices Buck's traces free.

Hal curses, grabs the gee-pole, and forces the remaining team onto the rotting river ice.

A hundred yards out, the rear of the sled drops into a fissure. Mercedes SCREAMS.

With a deafening roar, a whole section of ice collapses! Humans, dogs, and sled disappear into the churning dark water. A yawning hole is all that remains.

Thornton kneels beside Buck, examining him gently.

JOHN THORNTON
You poor devil.

Buck licks Thornton's hand.

EXT. THORNTON'S CAMP - DAY

Beside a sparkling river, SKEET, a little Irish setter, licks Buck's healing wounds. NIG, a giant black hound, rests his head nearby.

Buck is restored to prime strength—150 pounds of dense muscle and glowing fur.

Thornton takes Buck's head roughly between his hands, resting forehead to forehead, shaking him back and forth.

JOHN THORNTON
God! You can all but speak!

Buck takes Thornton's hand in his mouth, pressing his teeth into the flesh in an affectionate, feigned bite.

INT. ELDORADO SALOON - DAY

A crowded Dawson saloon packed with miners, sourdoughs, and gold scales.

MATTHEWSON, a wealthy Bonanza King, slams a heavy gold sack onto the wooden bar.

MATTHEWSON
My dog can start seven hundred pounds and walk off with it!

JOHN THORNTON
Pooh! Pooh! Buck can start a thousand pounds!

MATTHEWSON
And break it out? And walk off with it for a hundred yards?

JOHN THORNTON
And break it out, and walk off with it for a hundred yards.

MATTHEWSON
Well, I've got a thousand dollars that says he can't. And there it is.

Thornton pales, realizing the enormity of the claim. He looks at JIM O'BRIEN.

JOHN THORNTON
Can you lend me a thousand?

JIM O'BRIEN
Sure. Though it's little faith I'm having, John, that the beast can do the trick.

O'Brien drops a matching gold sack beside Matthewson's.

EXT. DAWSON STREET - DAY

Sixty below zero. A crowd of hundreds banks around a sled loaded with twenty 50-pound sacks of flour. The runners are frozen solid to the packed snow.

Buck is harnessed to the sled. SKOOKUM KING, a wealthy miner, marvels at Buck's muscular frame.

SKOOKUM KING
Gad, sir! Gad, sir! I offer you eight hundred for him, sir, before the test, sir! Eight hundred just as he stands!

Thornton shakes his head. He kneels beside Buck, taking his head in his hands.

JOHN THORNTON
As you love me, Buck. As you love me.

Buck gently grips Thornton's hand in his teeth, then releases it. Thornton steps back.

JOHN THORNTON
Now, Buck.

Buck tightens the traces, then slacks them back.

JOHN THORNTON
Gee!

Buck swerves right, lunging—CRACKLE! The right runner breaks free of the ice.

JOHN THORNTON
Haw!

Buck swerves left—SNAP! The left runner slips. The load is broken out!

JOHN THORNTON
Now, MUSH!

Buck throws his whole body forward! His chest drops low, claws scarring the hard snow.

The sled trembles... jerks... moves!

An inch... a foot... a yard! Buck gains momentum, hauling the half-ton load past the hundred-yard post!

The crowd bursts into a thunderous roar, throwing hats and mittens into the air!

Thornton falls on his knees beside Buck, weeping openly, hugging the dog's head.

SKOOKUM KING
I'll give you a thousand for him, sir! A thousand, sir—twelve hundred, sir!

JOHN THORNTON
Sir, no, sir. You can go to hell, sir. It's the best I can do for you, sir.

EXT. VIRGIN EAST VALLEY - DAY

A pristine valley where a shallow creek flows over glittering gold dust.

Moose-hide sacks filled with gold are stacked like logs outside a spruce-bough lodge.

Thornton, HANS, and PETE wash pans of gold in the stream.

Buck lies on a knoll, his head raised, listening to the vast forest.

A long, mournful wolf howl drifts through the trees.

EXT. VIRGIN EAST VALLEY - WOODS - NIGHT

Moonlight pierces the dark canopy.

Buck bounds silently through the forest, tracking the call.

In a moonlit glade, a long, lean TIMBER WOLF sits on his haunches, howling at the sky.

The wolf flees as Buck approaches, but Buck pursues until the wolf turns at bay. Buck circles with friendly overtures. The wolf sniffs Buck's muzzle.

Together, Buck and his wild brother run side by side through the night forest.

Buck halts suddenly, remembering John Thornton. He turns back toward camp as the wolf howls sorrowfully behind him.

EXT. VIRGIN EAST VALLEY - DESOLATED CAMP - DAY

Buck trots into the clearing and freezes, his mane bristling.

Nig lies dead in the brush, an arrow through his body. Hans lies face down, feathered with arrows.

YEEHAT INDIANS dance exultantly around the destroyed lodge.

Buck explodes into a roaring hurricane of vengeance!

He leaps at the Yeehat Chief, ripping his throat wide open in a jet of blood! He strikes a second hunter in mid-air, tearing his neck open!

Yeehats shoot arrows wildly, hitting their own men in terror.

YEEHAT HUNTER
The Evil Spirit!

The Yeehats scatter and flee into the forest in wild panic.

Buck walks slowly to the edge of a deep, muddy pool. Skeet lies dead at the water's edge.

Thornton's trail leads directly into the deep water—and no trail leads out.

Buck sits by the pool, head bowed, brooding in bitter solitude.

EXT. VIRGIN EAST VALLEY - CLEARING - NIGHT

A bright full moon illuminates the valley.

A silver flood of timber wolves pours into the clearing.

Buck stands in the center, massive and still as stone.

A bold wolf leaps—Buck strikes, breaking its neck! Three more attack—Buck gasps and slashes, backing into a right angle of a gravel bank to face the front.

After half an hour of ferocious battle, the wolves draw back discomfited, panting.

The lean timber wolf steps forward, whining softly, and touches noses with Buck.

An old gaunt wolf sits back and raises a long wolf howl. The whole pack joins in.

Buck sits on his haunches, points his nose to the moon, and howls—answering the call.

Buck bounds out from the bank, running side by side with his wild brother at the head of the pack under the shimmering northern lights.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And here may well end the story of Buck. When the long winter nights come on and the wolves follow their meat into the lower valleys, a Ghost Dog may be seen running at the head of the pack through the pale moonlight, leaping gigantic above his fellows, his great throat a-bellow as he sings a song of the younger world, which is the song of the pack.

FADE OUT.

THE END
